[[0,1434975,0,2,0,2,"2016-11-25T10:18:05","Limpingdead","",[],"<p>Everyone knows they advertise huge discounts on a specific item and usually only have 5 to 10 of these items in stock. Its why there is no &quot;rain check&quot; on every door buster. Should it be considered fraud for stores to overly advertise something they really do not have enough in stock just to drive business into the store?</p>","Should US stores be required to have a substantial number of \"door busters\"?","","AskVoat"],[1,6924617,0,0,1,-1,"2016-11-25T14:16:27","Biscuitbaiter","",[],"<p>Supply and demand. Just because they advertise they have something doesn't mean that they should have to supply it indefinitely. Most of those &quot;deals&quot; also come with the advertised small print of, &quot;While supplies last&quot; or &quot;At participating locations&quot; notifying people that these items are possibly in demand and of limited quantities. The amount of money something can generate in part depends on that demand. If everyone can get something somewhere it wouldn't be a valuable item and wouldn't be justifiable for generating the sales needed to own a spot on a shelf. </p>"],[1,6934043,0,0,1,-1,"2016-11-26T03:29:37","Teflon-jersey","",[],"<p>Stop being a materialistic cuck. </p>"]]
